  "response": "Ran `crimes context src/billing.ts`: **HIGH risk — 11 findings (3 high, 6 medium, 2 low)**, test gap bottom-quartile, 1 lapsed suppression. I read the file to ground the ranking.\n\n## 1. Break up `generateInvoice` — starting with the duplicated tax table\n\n**Findings:** God Function (`large_function`, sev 0.9, conf 0.95, lines 50–253 — 3.4× the 60-line domain threshold), God File (310 lines).\n\n204 lines mixing pricing, loyalty discounts, promo codes, fraud scoring, tax, currency, PII logging, metrics, email rendering, and a retry loop. But the concrete reason to act isn't length — it's what the length hides:\n\n- `src/billing.ts:144-154` is a country→rate `if/else` chain **character-identical** to the exported `tax()` at `src/billing.ts:254-267`. Nothing links them. Change GB VAT in `tax()` and `generateInvoice` silently keeps charging the old rate on every invoice.\n- `daysSince` at line 92 is duplicated in `recommendUpgrade` (line 273) — the file's own comment admits it.\n- Currency selection (`:160-166`) is a third country-keyed policy table with no shared authority.\n\nThe fix isn't \"split the file\" — it's extract `taxRateFor(country)`, `currencyFor(country)`, `planDiscount`, `promoDiscount`, `riskScore` as pure functions and have both call sites use them. That's `effort: small` per the scan, it's the only way to get tests on any of it, and it converts the God File finding into a natural follow-up rather than a speculative reorg.\n\n## 2. Fix `calculateRefundPreview` — the name lies and the auth rule isn't code\n\n**Findings:** False Identity (`name_behavior_mismatch`, conf 0.80), Logic in the Alibi (conf 0.74), Commented-Out Corpse (conf 0.90) — all stacked on lines 33–44.\n\nEleven lines carrying three separate traps:\n\n- The function is named `calculate…Preview` but `await`s `saveRefundAudit()` and `sendRefundEmail()`. Any caller — human or agent — reasonably treats it as pure and safe to call in a render path or a loop. It emails the customer.\n- The comment on line 39 says *\"Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves.\"* The body checks **nothing** — no role, no plan, no approval. That's an authorization rule that exists only as prose, sitting directly above a function that writes an audit row and sends mail. This is the finding I'd treat as a potential security issue, not a style nit.\n- Lines 33–37 are commented-out refund code calling `loadRefund`/`approveRefund`/`sendRefundReceipt`. An agent looking for \"how refunds work here\" has a stale implementation as the most prominent answer in the file.\n\nSplit it into a pure `refundPreviewCents()` plus an explicit `issueRefund()`, encode the owner/enterprise/approval rule as a guard that throws, and delete the corpse (git has it).\n\n## 3. Export the canonical `User` / `Invoice` — the split-brain is a symptom, not the disease\n\n**Findings:** Contract Split-Brain ×2 (sev 0.96 and 0.90, conf 0.82) — the highest-severity findings in the whole scan.\n\n`Invoice` in `src/billing.ts:19` has 11 fields; `Invoice` in `src/api/state.ts:9` has 2. Every money field is missing from the second copy: `subtotalCents`, `discountCents`, `taxCents`, `currency`, plus `userId` and `createdAt`. Same story for `User` (5 fields vs 2, `createdAt` missing).\n\nThe root cause is visible in the source: **`interface User` and `interface Invoice` in billing.ts are not exported**, yet `generateInvoice`, `calculateRefundPreview`, and `listRecentInvoices` all are. Consumers literally cannot name the types of this module's public API, so they re-declare them — which is exactly how the `state.ts` stub came to exist. Export them (or lift them to a shared `types.ts`) and have the other declaration derive via `Pick<Invoice, …>`, so the next field change reaches both sides through the type checker.\n\n---\n\n**Close fourth, worth folding into #1:** the retry loop at `:239-248` catches with no binding, no logging, no rethrow (`swallowed_error`, conf 0.78). It runs 3 attempts, writes `metadata.emailAttempts = \"3\"`, and returns the invoice as a success. A caller cannot distinguish \"emailed\" from \"failed three times\". Since you'll be touching that region while extracting helpers anyway, that's the moment to return a typed send result.\n\n**One caveat on sequencing:** `src/billing.test.ts` gives you no cover for any of this — one test has zero assertions and the other asserts `toBeTruthy()` on a local stub (`weak_test_signal` ×2). The extractions in #1 are behaviour-preserving by construction, so do them first, then pin the tax and discount tables with real assertions before anything reshapes the money math. Also note the scan flagged a **lapsed suppression** on this file — a pin written pre-0.8.0 stopped matching, so something previously accepted is live again unsilenced; `crimes audit-suppressions` before you start.",
